
/* =============== 5.2 flashcards & mnemonics =============== */
const PARTN={1:"Part 1 · The disease",2:"Part 2 · Course and diagnosis",3:"Part 3 · Treatment",4:"Part 4 · Living, caring, preventing",5:"Part 5 · History"};
const FC=[
[1,'Is dementia the same thing as Alzheimer\'s disease?','No. Dementia is a clinical syndrome: cognitive problems that interfere with everyday tasks such as driving, cooking or cleaning. Alzheimer\'s disease is its most common cause. Other causes include cerebrovascular disease, frontotemporal degeneration and Lewy body disease.'],
[1,'What share of dementia is due to Alzheimer\'s disease?','The sources differ. The Alzheimer\'s Association estimates 60% to 80% of dementia cases, while the WHO fact sheet gives 60% to 70%. Most people with Alzheimer\'s also have the brain changes of another cause (mixed pathology).'],
[1,'How many people were living with dementia worldwide in 2021, according to WHO?','57 million, over 60% of them in low- and middle-income countries. WHO counts nearly 10 million new cases each year.'],
[1,'How many older Americans had Alzheimer\'s dementia in 2025?','An estimated 7.2 million people aged 65 and older (Alzheimer\'s Association 2025). That is about 1 in 9 people in that age group, and almost two-thirds are women.'],
[1,'In the Rush Memory and Aging Project autopsies, how often was Alzheimer\'s the only pathology in people with dementia?','In 30%. The most common finding was Alzheimer\'s plus infarcts (38%), and more than half had several diagnoses. Also, 24.2% of people without dementia had pure Alzheimer\'s pathology.'],
[1,'Which enzyme starts the amyloidogenic pathway, and which one prevents Aβ from forming?','BACE1 (beta-secretase) cuts APP at the start of the Aβ sequence, leaving the C99 fragment. Alpha-secretase (ADAM10) cuts inside the Aβ sequence, so no Aβ can be made.'],
[1,'What is gamma-secretase made of?','Four components: presenilin (PS1 or PS2, the catalytic subunit), nicastrin, APH-1 and PEN-2. It cuts C99 inside the membrane to release Aβ and the intracellular domain AICD.'],
[1,'Aβ40 or Aβ42: which is more common, and which is stickier?','Aβ40 makes up most of the Aβ produced. Aβ42 is only about 10%, but it is more hydrophobic, forms fibrils more readily and is the main plaque component. Vessel-wall amyloid (CAA) is enriched in Aβ40.'],
[1,'In what order does Aβ aggregate?','Monomer, then oligomer, then protofibril, then fibril, then plaque. Soluble oligomers are thought to drive synapse damage, while large plaques may act as reservoirs.'],
[1,'How do diffuse plaques differ from neuritic plaques?','Diffuse plaques stain weakly and lack damaged neurites and activated glia. Neuritic plaques are dense-core plaques surrounded by swollen tau-positive neurites, activated microglia, reactive astrocytes and synapse loss.'],
[1,'What does tau normally do?','Tau is a neuronal protein, concentrated in axons, that helps tubulin assemble into microtubules and keeps them stable. It is encoded by MAPT on chromosome 17, and the adult brain makes six isoforms (3R and 4R).'],
[1,'What is a neurofibrillary tangle made of?','Hyperphosphorylated tau assembled into paired helical filaments, two filaments twisted around each other, bundled inside the neuron. When the neuron dies, an extracellular ghost tangle is left behind.'],
[1,'What are the Braak stages of tangle spread?','Stages I-II: transentorhinal cortex. Stages III-IV: limbic regions, with the entorhinal cortex fully involved and CA1 and subiculum affected. Stages V-VI: association isocortex, and finally even primary sensory cortex.'],
[1,'Where does amyloid deposit first in the Thal phases?','In the neocortex (phase 1). It then spreads to the allocortex (2), the diencephalon, striatum and basal forebrain (3), the brainstem (4) and the cerebellum (5). Tangles, by contrast, start in the transentorhinal cortex.'],
[1,'Which correlates best with how severe the cognitive problems are: plaques, tangles or synapses?','Plaque burden correlates only weakly. Neocortical tangles correlate better, and in a classic 1991 study neocortical synapse density correlated most strongly of all.'],
[1,'Why does episodic memory usually fail first?','The first tangles and neuron loss hit entorhinal layer II, which sends the perforant path into the hippocampus, and then CA1 and the subiculum. This cuts the hippocampus off from its inputs and outputs.'],
[1,'What happens to the nucleus basalis of Meynert in Alzheimer\'s disease?','Its neurons, the main cholinergic input to the cortex, degenerate profoundly (more than 75%). This is the basis of the cortical acetylcholine deficit that cholinesterase inhibitors target.'],
[1,'What is TREM2, and what did the R47H variant show?','TREM2 is a receptor on microglia. The rare R47H variant raised Alzheimer\'s risk about 2.9-fold in Icelanders. Without TREM2, microglia fail to wrap around plaques, which then have more neuritic damage around them.'],
[1,'Which three genes cause autosomal dominant Alzheimer\'s disease, and on which chromosomes are they?','APP on chromosome 21, PSEN1 on chromosome 14 and PSEN2 on chromosome 1. Sources disagree on how common this form is: fewer than 1% of all cases (Bateman 2011) or under 5% (ACMG/NSGC guideline).'],
[1,'How much does APOE ε4 raise Alzheimer\'s risk?','A Nature Reviews summary puts it at about 3- to 4-fold for one copy and 9- to 15-fold for two. ε4 raises risk but does not decide the outcome: many ε4/ε4 people never develop dementia. ε2 is protective.'],
[1,'Why do almost all adults with Down syndrome show Alzheimer\'s pathology by age 40?','APP lies on chromosome 21, so a third copy of the chromosome means extra APP. A woman with partial trisomy 21 and only two copies of APP showed no Alzheimer\'s disease at 78.'],
[1,'What did Nature retract in 2024?','The 2006 paper by Lesne and colleagues reporting a 56-kDa Aβ assembly (Aβ*56) in mice. The retraction removes one paper\'s evidence for one oligomer species in one mouse model. Other evidence for the amyloid hypothesis, such as human genetics and biomarker timelines, does not rest on it.'],
[2,'In the hypothetical biomarker cascade, what changes first?','Amyloid markers: CSF Aβ42 falls and amyloid PET retention rises while the person is still cognitively normal. Neurodegeneration markers (CSF tau, atrophy on MRI, lower FDG-PET uptake) follow after a lag, and they track symptom severity.'],
[2,'What did DIAN show about timing in dominantly inherited Alzheimer\'s disease?','Relative to expected onset: CSF Aβ42 began to fall about 25 years before, amyloid PET, CSF tau and atrophy about 15 years before, hypometabolism and episodic memory about 10 years before, and global cognition about 5 years before. Dementia criteria were met about 3 years after.'],
[2,'What is a Core 1 biomarker in the Alzheimer\'s Association 2024 criteria?','A marker that becomes abnormal around the same time as amyloid PET: amyloid PET itself, approved CSF assays, or accurate plasma assays, especially p-tau217. Under these criteria one abnormal Core 1 result is enough to establish the diagnosis.'],
[2,'How does the International Working Group (IWG 2024) disagree with the Alzheimer\'s Association 2024 criteria?','The IWG rejects a purely biological definition for clinical use. It says cognitively normal people who are only amyloid-positive should be described as at risk for Alzheimer\'s, not as having it. Both groups advise against testing unimpaired people outside research.'],
[2,'What are the biological stages A to D in the 2024 criteria?','A: amyloid PET positive with no tau PET uptake. B: tau uptake in the medial temporal lobe. C: moderate neocortical tau. D: high neocortical tau.'],
[2,'What are the clinical stages 0 to 6 in the 2024 criteria?','0: carrier of a deterministic gene, biomarkers normal. 1: no symptoms, biomarkers abnormal. 2: transitional decline. 3: cognitive impairment with early functional impact. 4: mild dementia. 5: moderate dementia. 6: severe dementia.'],
[2,'What do A, T and (N) stand for in the 2018 research framework?','A: amyloid (CSF Aβ42 or the Aβ42/40 ratio, or amyloid PET). T: tau (CSF phosphorylated tau or tau PET). (N): neurodegeneration (MRI, FDG-PET or CSF total tau), in parentheses because it is not specific to Alzheimer\'s.'],
[2,'What are the FAST stage 6 substages?','6a needs help dressing, 6b bathing, 6c toileting mechanics, 6d urinary incontinence, 6e faecal incontinence.'],
[2,'What are the FAST stage 7 substages?','7a speech limited to a few words a day, 7b one intelligible word, 7c loss of walking, 7d loss of sitting up, 7e loss of smiling, 7f loss of the ability to hold up the head.'],
[2,'Which six domains does the Clinical Dementia Rating score?','Memory; Orientation; Judgment and Problem Solving; Community Affairs; Home and Hobbies; Personal Care. Each box is rated from a semi-structured interview with the person and an informant.'],
[2,'How do CDR Sum of Boxes scores map onto severity?','CDR-SB runs from 0 to 18. 0.5-4.0 matches CDR 0.5 (questionable or very mild), 4.5-9.0 mild, 9.5-15.5 moderate and 16.0-18.0 severe.'],
[2,'How are MMSE scores conventionally mapped onto severity?','On a 30-point test: 30 none, 26-29 questionable, 21-25 mild, 11-20 moderate, 0-10 severe. Agreement with CDR stages was poor for separating normal from questionable.'],
[2,'What is the MoCA cut-off?','It is disputed. The original validation and the owner\'s FAQ use 26 or above as normal, with one point added for 12 or fewer years of education. A 2018 meta-analysis found 23 gave the best accuracy for MCI, because 26 inflates false positives in older and less-educated people.'],
[2,'What was the first FDA-cleared blood test to aid Alzheimer\'s diagnosis?','The Lumipulse G pTau217/β-Amyloid 1-42 Plasma Ratio, cleared on 16 May 2025 for adults 55 and older with signs and symptoms of cognitive decline in specialised care. The FDA says it is not a screening or stand-alone diagnostic test.'],
[2,'What accuracy does the Alzheimer\'s Association 2025 guideline ask of blood tests?','At least 90% sensitivity and 75% specificity for triage, with a positive result confirmed by PET or CSF. At least 90% for both to replace PET or CSF. Both are conditional recommendations for specialised care only.'],
[2,'What are the main atypical (non-amnestic) presentations of Alzheimer\'s disease?','Posterior cortical atrophy (visual and spatial problems), logopenic variant primary progressive aphasia (word-finding pauses, poor sentence repetition) and frontal variants. They make up about one-third of young-onset cases but about 6% of late-onset cases.'],
[2,'What are the four core clinical features of dementia with Lewy bodies?','Fluctuating cognition, recurrent well-formed visual hallucinations, REM sleep behaviour disorder and parkinsonism (2017 consensus).'],
[2,'How long do people live after a diagnosis of Alzheimer\'s dementia?','People aged 65 and older survive on average 4 to 8 years, and some live 20 years. Age matters: median survival was 8.3 years when diagnosed at 65 and 3.4 years at 90. Pneumonia is the most commonly identified immediate cause of death.'],
[2,'How often does mild cognitive impairment progress to dementia?','In a meta-analysis of 41 cohorts, about 9.6% a year in specialist clinics and 4.9% a year in community studies. Most people with MCI had not progressed even after 10 years.'],
[3,'How do cholinesterase inhibitors work?','They block the breakdown of acetylcholine in the synaptic cleft, so more acetylcholine reaches the receptors. The labels state there is no evidence that they change the course of the underlying disease.'],
[3,'How do donepezil, rivastigmine and galantamine differ?','Donepezil selectively inhibits AChE and has a half-life of about 70 hours. Rivastigmine is a pseudo-irreversible inhibitor of both AChE and BuChE. Galantamine is a competitive, reversible AChE inhibitor with a half-life of about 7 hours.'],
[3,'Which cholinesterase inhibitors are labelled for severe Alzheimer\'s dementia in the US?','Donepezil (severe disease added on 13 October 2006) and the rivastigmine patch. Oral rivastigmine and galantamine ER are labelled for mild-to-moderate disease.'],
[3,'How large is the benefit of donepezil?','Cochrane 2018, 10 mg a day at 26 weeks: ADAS-Cog 2.67 points better on a 0-70 scale and MMSE about 1 point better than placebo. The reviews describe the benefit as small.'],
[3,'What are the most common side effects of cholinesterase inhibitors?','Gastrointestinal: nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea and loss of appetite, mostly during dose increases. The labels also warn about slow heart rate, heart block and fainting. Oral rivastigmine caused nausea in 47% versus 12% on placebo.'],
[3,'What kind of drug is memantine?','A low-to-moderate-affinity, uncompetitive (open-channel) NMDA receptor antagonist, labelled for moderate-to-severe Alzheimer\'s dementia. A Cochrane review (2019) found no benefit in mild disease (MMSE 20-23).'],
[3,'What does lecanemab bind, and what did CLARITY AD show?','Lecanemab binds soluble Aβ protofibrils with high affinity. Over 18 months, CDR-SB worsened by 1.21 points on lecanemab versus 1.66 on placebo, a difference of 0.45 points (27% slowing).'],
[3,'What does donanemab bind, and what did TRAILBLAZER-ALZ 2 show?','Donanemab binds N-terminal pyroglutamate Aβ (N3pG), found only in deposited plaque. At 76 weeks the CDR-SB difference was 0.70 points in the combined population (29% slowing). Dosing switched to placebo once amyloid was cleared.'],
[3,'What is the difference between ARIA-E and ARIA-H?','ARIA-E is vasogenic oedema or sulcal effusion seen on FLAIR MRI, and usually resolves. ARIA-H is microhaemorrhage or superficial siderosis seen on T2* or susceptibility-weighted MRI, and persists.'],
[3,'How common was ARIA-E in the pivotal antibody trials?','Lecanemab 12.6% versus 1.7% on placebo (CLARITY AD). Donanemab 24.0% versus 2.1% (TRAILBLAZER-ALZ 2). Risk was highest in APOE ε4 homozygotes.'],
[3,'Why do the antibody labels mention thrombolytic drugs?','ARIA-E can mimic a stroke, and fatal brain haemorrhages have followed thrombolysis in antibody-treated patients. The labels advise considering ARIA-E before a thrombolytic is given.'],
[3,'Is the benefit of the anti-amyloid antibodies clinically meaningful?','This is debated. A 2024 meta-analysis found that none of the antibodies exceeded the minimal clinically important difference. Other authors argue that within-person thresholds (about 1 to 2 CDR-SB points) cannot be compared with differences between trial groups. NICE judged the benefits modest and the costs too high; the FDA approved both drugs.'],
[3,'What does the boxed warning on antipsychotics in dementia say?','Pooled analyses of 17 placebo-controlled trials showed a 1.6- to 1.7-fold higher risk of death, about 4.5% versus 2.6% over 10 weeks. Most deaths were cardiovascular or infectious.'],
[3,'What is brexpiprazole approved for?','Agitation associated with dementia due to Alzheimer\'s disease (FDA, 11 May 2023), the first drug approved for this. It keeps the class boxed warning on mortality, and the label says it is not for as-needed use.'],
[3,'What does DICE stand for?','Describe, Investigate, Create, Evaluate: a caregiver-centred approach to behavioural symptoms that looks for causes (unmet needs, medical problems, caregiver and environmental factors) before considering drugs.'],
[3,'Which neuropsychiatric symptoms are most common in Alzheimer\'s disease?','In a meta-analysis using the Neuropsychiatric Inventory: apathy 49%, depression 42%, aggression 40%, anxiety 39% and sleep disorder 39%. In a population study, 75% of people with dementia had a symptom in the past month.'],
[3,'What did the CitAD trial of citalopram for agitation find?','Agitation improved, but MMSE fell by about 1 point and the QTc interval lengthened by 18.1 ms. The 30 mg dose used is above the labelled maximum of 20 mg a day for people over 60.'],
[4,'How many modifiable risk factors did the 2024 Lancet Commission list, and what share of dementia do they account for?','14 factors, together about 45% of dementia worldwide. This is a modelled upper bound that assumes the factors cause dementia and could be removed completely. It is not what any trial has achieved.'],
[4,'Which risk factors were added in 2024, and which contribute most?','Untreated vision loss and high LDL cholesterol were new. Hearing impairment and high LDL cholesterol are the largest single contributors, about 7% each.'],
[4,'What did the FINGER multidomain lifestyle trial find?','A small benefit: the neuropsychological test score improved 0.022 per year more with diet, exercise, cognitive training and vascular monitoring than with general advice. Other multidomain trials (preDIVA, MAPT) were null.'],
[4,'What did SPRINT MIND find about intensive blood pressure control?','A target below 120 mm Hg did not significantly reduce probable dementia (HR 0.83, 95% CI 0.67-1.04), but it did reduce mild cognitive impairment (HR 0.81). The trial stopped early and may have been underpowered.'],
[4,'Did hearing aids slow cognitive decline in ACHIEVE?','Not overall: the primary result was null. In a higher-risk subgroup from the ARIC cohort, decline was slowed by 0.19 SD over 3 years. A subgroup result supports the idea but does not prove it.'],
[4,'What did the Welsh shingles vaccine natural experiment find?','Eligibility depended on date of birth. Vaccination reduced new dementia diagnoses over 7 years by 3.5 percentage points, a 20% relative reduction. Similar results came from Australia and Canada, but no randomised trial has tested it yet.'],
[4,'How much unpaid care do US dementia caregivers give?','In 2024, 11.9 million family members and other unpaid caregivers gave about 19.2 billion hours, valued at $413.5 billion, nearly 31 hours per caregiver per week.'],
[4,'How common is depression among dementia caregivers?','The Alzheimer\'s Association reports 30% to 40%. A meta-analysis of caregivers of people with Alzheimer\'s found 34.0%, more often in female and spousal caregivers.'],
[4,'Which caregiver programme delayed nursing-home placement?','The NYU Caregiver Intervention (counselling, support groups and on-call telephone help for spouses) reduced the rate of placement by 28.3%, with model-predicted median placement 557 days later.'],
[4,'What does the American Geriatrics Society say about feeding tubes in advanced dementia?','They are not recommended (2014 position statement). Careful hand feeding is considered as good as tube feeding for death, aspiration pneumonia, function and comfort.'],
[4,'What did the CASCADE study show about advanced dementia in nursing homes?','Over 18 months 54.8% died. Eating problems affected 85.8%, febrile episodes 52.6% and pneumonia 41.1%. When proxies understood the prognosis, burdensome interventions were much less likely.'],
[4,'What are the four items of the 4AT?','Alertness (0 or 4), AMT4 (0-2), Attention by months backwards (0-2) and Acute change or fluctuating course (0 or 4). A total of 4 or more suggests delirium; 1-3 suggests cognitive impairment.'],
[4,'What are the five PAINAD items?','Breathing independent of vocalisation, negative vocalisation, facial expression, body language and consolability, each scored 0-2 for a total of 0-10.'],
[4,'What does the Exelon patch label say about applying the rivastigmine patch?','One patch at a time, replaced every 24 hours after removing the old one, preferably on the upper or lower back (otherwise upper arm or chest). Do not reuse a site within 14 days or cut the patch. Most serious errors involved the old patch left on or several worn at once.'],
[4,'What process does NICE SC1 describe for covert medication in care homes?','Medicines should not be hidden from a resident who has capacity. Otherwise: a capacity assessment, a best-interests meeting (staff, prescriber, pharmacist, family or advocate), documented reasons, an administration plan and regular review.'],
[4,'What is an ABC chart?','A record of the Antecedent (what happened before), the Behaviour and the Consequence (what happened after). ABC recording is the basis of functional analysis of behaviour.'],
[4,'What happened in the main prevention trials in people without symptoms?','Solanezumab (A4) and crenezumab (API Colombia), antibodies that do not clear plaque well, showed no slowing. Trials of plaque-clearing antibodies before symptoms (AHEAD 3-45, TRAILBLAZER-ALZ 3) are still running.'],
[5,'Who was Auguste D.?','Alois Alzheimer\'s patient, admitted to the Frankfurt psychiatric hospital in November 1901 (usually given as 25 November). She stayed there until her death on 8 April 1906. Her case file was rediscovered in Frankfurt in December 1995.'],
[5,'When and where did Alzheimer first describe the disease?','On 3 November 1906, at the 37th Meeting of South-West German Psychiatrists in Tübingen. He described plaques and neurofibrillary tangles, and the talk drew little interest. He published the case in 1907.'],
[5,'Who named Alzheimer\'s disease?','Emil Kraepelin, in the 8th edition of his textbook Psychiatrie (1910). The split between presenile Alzheimer\'s disease and senile dementia was dropped at a 1978 meeting in Bethesda.'],
[5,'What did Blessed, Tomlinson and Roth show in 1968?','Using quantitative methods in Newcastle, they showed that plaque counts correlated with dementia severity, and that plaques and tangles also occurred in older people without dementia.'],
[5,'Where did the cholinergic hypothesis come from?','Davies and Maloney (1976) reported a selective loss of central cholinergic neurons in Alzheimer\'s disease. Bartus and colleagues set out the cholinergic hypothesis of geriatric memory dysfunction in 1982.'],
[5,'What did Glenner and Wong report in 1984?','They purified and sequenced a new cerebrovascular amyloid protein from Alzheimer\'s brains, later called amyloid-beta.'],
[5,'What did Grundke-Iqbal and colleagues show in 1986?','That tau is an abnormally phosphorylated component of the paired helical filaments in tangles.'],
[5,'What happened in 1987 and 1991 in amyloid genetics?','In 1987 Kang and colleagues cloned the amyloid precursor and placed its gene on chromosome 21. In 1991 Goate and colleagues found the first APP missense mutation segregating with familial Alzheimer\'s disease.'],
[5,'Who proposed the amyloid cascade hypothesis, and when?','Hardy and Higgins, in Science in 1992, among several papers from 1991-1992. It became the dominant model of how the disease develops.'],
[5,'What did the 1993 APOE studies find?','Strittmatter and colleagues found an ε4 allele frequency of 0.50 in familial late-onset Alzheimer\'s versus 0.16 in controls. Corder and colleagues showed a gene-dose effect: risk rose from 20% to 90% and mean onset fell from 84 to 68 with more ε4 copies.'],
[5,'When were the first symptomatic Alzheimer\'s drugs approved by the FDA?','Tacrine (Cognex) on 9 September 1993, the first. Donepezil (Aricept) on 25 November 1996. Memantine (Namenda) on 16 October 2003.'],
[5,'What happened to aducanumab?','It received FDA accelerated approval on 7 June 2021, based on amyloid reduction, after an advisory committee had voted in November 2020 that the evidence was insufficient. On 31 January 2024 Biogen announced it was ending the drug\'s development and sales.'],
[5,'When were lecanemab and donanemab approved in the US?','Lecanemab received accelerated approval on 6 January 2023 and traditional approval on 6 July 2023, the first amyloid antibody to convert. Donanemab was approved on 2 July 2024.']
];
const MN=[
['The biomarker cascade','<b>Amyloid first, symptoms last.</b> Amyloid markers (CSF Aβ42 falling, amyloid PET rising) turn abnormal while thinking is still normal. Tau PET and neurodegeneration markers (atrophy, FDG-PET) follow, closer to symptoms. In dominantly inherited disease, count down the years before expected onset: <b>25</b> CSF Aβ42 falls, <b>15</b> amyloid PET, CSF tau and atrophy, <b>10</b> hypometabolism and episodic memory, <b>5</b> global cognition, then dementia about 3 years after. The DIAN authors caution that these timings may not apply to sporadic disease.'],
['Braak stages I to VI: T, L, I','<b>Two stages per region, moving outward.</b> I-II <b>T</b>ransentorhinal, III-IV <b>L</b>imbic (entorhinal cortex, CA1, subiculum), V-VI <b>I</b>socortical (association cortex, then even primary areas). A memory aid; it describes tangles, not plaques.'],
['Thal phases 1 to 5: N, A, D, B, C','<b>Amyloid starts on the outside and sinks down.</b> 1 <b>N</b>eocortex, 2 <b>A</b>llocortex (hippocampus, entorhinal), 3 <b>D</b>eep grey matter (diencephalon, striatum, basal forebrain), 4 <b>B</b>rainstem, 5 <b>C</b>erebellum. Contrast with tau, which starts deep in the transentorhinal cortex. A memory aid, not a staging tool.'],
['Dominant genes: 21, 14, 1','<b>Count down: APP 21, PSEN1 14, PSEN2 1.</b> APP sits on chromosome 21, the same chromosome tripled in Down syndrome. PSEN1 mutations give the youngest onsets (typically 30 to 50), APP families usually begin in their 50s, and PSEN2 has a wide range with some later onsets.'],
['APOE: 2 protects, 3 is the reference, 4 raises risk','<b>ε2</b> lowers risk, <b>ε3</b> is the comparison genotype, <b>ε4</b> raises it: about 3- to 4-fold for one copy and 9- to 15-fold for two. Risk is not destiny: many ε4 homozygotes never develop dementia, and most APOE alleles in people with Alzheimer\'s are not ε4. ε4 also raises ARIA risk on antibodies.'],
['ARIA-E versus ARIA-H','<b>E for Edema, H for Haemosiderin.</b> ARIA-E: vasogenic oedema or sulcal effusion, seen on FLAIR, usually resolves. ARIA-H: microhaemorrhages or superficial siderosis, seen on T2* or SWI, persists. Both are commoner in APOE ε4 homozygotes, and ARIA-E mostly appears in the first months of treatment.'],
['PAINAD\'s five items','<b>Breathing, Noise, Face, Body, Comfort.</b> Breathing independent of vocalisation, Negative vocalisation, Facial expression, Body language, Consolability: five items scored 0-2, total 0-10. A memory aid, not a clinical tool. The widely reproduced severity bands have not been validated for this scale.'],
['The 4AT: four As','<b>Alertness, AMT4, Attention, Acute change.</b> Scores: Alertness 0 or 4, AMT4 0-2, Attention (months backwards) 0-2, Acute change or fluctuation 0 or 4. A total of 4 or more suggests delirium. Caution: in severe dementia, items 2 and 3 alone can reach 4 without delirium.'],
['CAM: 1 + 2 + (3 or 4)','Delirium on the Confusion Assessment Method needs <b>1</b> acute onset with fluctuating course <b>and 2</b> inattention, plus <b>either 3</b> disorganised thinking <b>or 4</b> altered level of consciousness.'],
['DICE for behaviour','<b>D</b>escribe the behaviour, <b>I</b>nvestigate causes (unmet needs, medical problems, caregiver and environmental factors), <b>C</b>reate a plan, <b>E</b>valuate whether it worked. The evidence for DICE itself is still limited.'],
['Lancet 2024 risk factors: 1, 10, 3','<b>Early life 1:</b> less education. <b>Midlife 10:</b> hearing loss, high LDL cholesterol, depression, head injury, inactivity, diabetes, smoking, hypertension, obesity, excessive alcohol. <b>Late life 3:</b> social isolation, air pollution, untreated vision loss. Together about 45%, a modelled upper bound that assumes the factors are causal.'],
['Rivastigmine patch: old off, one on, back first','As the label describes: remove yesterday\'s patch, apply <b>one</b> patch every <b>24</b> hours, preferably on the upper or lower <b>back</b> (otherwise upper arm or chest), do not reuse the same spot for <b>14</b> days and never cut it. Most serious errors involved the old patch left on or several worn at once.']
];
inits.review=()=>{
  let filt='all',deck=[],i=0;
  const F=[['all','All cards'],...Object.keys(PARTN).map(k=>[k,'Part '+k])];
  $('#fcFilter').innerHTML=F.map(([k,l])=>`<button class="btn" data-ff="${k}" aria-pressed="${k==='all'}">${l}</button>`).join('');
  const fc=$('#fc');
  function build(shuf){deck=FC.filter(c=>filt==='all'||String(c[0])===filt);if(shuf){const r=rng(Date.now()%1e6);for(let k=deck.length-1;k>0;k--){const j=Math.floor(r()*(k+1));[deck[k],deck[j]]=[deck[j],deck[k]]}}i=0;render()}
  function render(){const c=deck[i];fc.classList.remove('flip');
    $('#fcTagF').textContent=PARTN[c[0]]+' · question';$('#fcTagB').textContent=PARTN[c[0]]+' · answer';
    $('#fcFront').textContent=c[1];$('#fcBack').textContent=c[2];$('#fcCount').textContent=`${i+1} / ${deck.length}`;$('#fcBar').style.width=((i+1)/deck.length*100)+'%'}
  const flip=()=>fc.classList.toggle('flip');
  fc.addEventListener('click',flip);$('#fcFlip').onclick=flip;
  const next=()=>{i=(i+1)%deck.length;render()},prev=()=>{i=(i-1+deck.length)%deck.length;render()};
  $('#fcNext').onclick=next;$('#fcPrev').onclick=prev;$('#fcShuf').onclick=()=>build(true);
  $('#fcFilter').addEventListener('click',e=>{const b=e.target.closest('[data-ff]');if(!b)return;filt=b.dataset.ff;$$('[data-ff]').forEach(x=>x.setAttribute('aria-pressed',x===b));build(false)});
  document.addEventListener('keydown',e=>{if(cur!=='review')return;if(e.target.closest('input')||(e.target.closest('button')&&e.target!==fc))return;
    if(e.key===' '||e.key==='Enter'){e.preventDefault();flip()}else if(e.key==='ArrowRight')next();else if(e.key==='ArrowLeft')prev()});
  build(false);
  $('#mnList').innerHTML=MN.map(m=>`<div class="card"><b class="big">${m[0]}</b><p>${m[1]}</p></div>`).join('');
};

/* =============== 5.3 quiz =============== */
const QZ=[
[1,'Which enzyme makes the cut that starts the amyloidogenic processing of APP?',['ADAM10 (alpha-secretase)','Neprilysin','Acetylcholinesterase','BACE1 (beta-secretase)'],3,'BACE1 cuts APP at the start of the Aβ sequence, leaving C99. ADAM10 cuts inside Aβ, which prevents Aβ from forming, and neprilysin breaks Aβ down.'],
[1,'Which component of gamma-secretase is its catalytic subunit?',['Nicastrin','Presenilin','APH-1','PEN-2'],1,'Gamma-secretase has four components; presenilin (PS1 or PS2) carries the two essential aspartates that do the cutting.'],
[1,'Aβ42 is only about 10% of the Aβ produced. Why does it matter so much?',['It is the main component of vessel-wall amyloid','It is the form that clears fastest','It is more prone to form fibrils and is the main plaque component','It is made only by microglia'],2,'Aβ42 is more hydrophobic and fibrillogenic and can seed aggregation of Aβ40. Vessel-wall amyloid (CAA) is enriched in Aβ40 instead.'],
[1,'Which sequence describes Aβ aggregation?',['Plaque, fibril, protofibril, oligomer, monomer','Monomer, fibril, oligomer, plaque, protofibril','Oligomer, monomer, plaque, fibril, protofibril','Monomer, oligomer, protofibril, fibril, plaque'],3,'Monomers form small soluble oligomers, which lengthen into protofibrils, mature into fibrils and deposit as plaques.'],
[1,'A pathologist sees plaques with dense cores surrounded by swollen tau-positive neurites, activated microglia and reactive astrocytes. What are these?',['Neuritic plaques','Diffuse plaques','Ghost tangles','Lewy bodies'],0,'Neuritic plaques are the dense-core subset with dystrophic neurites and glial activation. Diffuse plaques lack damaged neurites and activated glia.'],
[1,'A stable-isotope study in late-onset Alzheimer\'s disease compared Aβ production and clearance with controls. What did it find?',['Production was doubled and clearance unchanged','Both production and clearance were faster','Production was unchanged and clearance was about 30% slower','Neither production nor clearance differed'],2,'Aβ42 production was 6.6 versus 6.7%/h, while clearance was 5.3 versus 7.6%/h, roughly a 30% impairment.'],
[1,'Braak stages III and IV describe tangles in which regions?',['The transentorhinal cortex only','Most of the association isocortex','The cerebellum and brainstem','Limbic regions, including the entorhinal cortex, CA1 and subiculum'],3,'Braak I-II is transentorhinal, III-IV limbic, and V-VI isocortical.'],
[1,'Where are amyloid deposits found in Thal phase 1?',['Transentorhinal cortex','Cerebellum','Neocortex','Brainstem nuclei'],2,'Amyloid starts in the neocortex and spreads inward and downward, reaching the cerebellum in phase 5. Tangles, in contrast, start in the transentorhinal cortex.'],
[1,'In autopsy studies, which measure tracks the severity of dementia most closely?',['Neocortical tangle burden','Total plaque count','Number of diffuse plaques','Amount of vessel-wall amyloid'],0,'Dementia severity related to neocortical tangle counts but not plaque counts, and a multi-centre consensus review reached the same conclusion.'],
[1,'Tau is encoded by MAPT. On which chromosome is it?',['Chromosome 17','Chromosome 21','Chromosome 14','Chromosome 1'],0,'MAPT is on chromosome 17. Chromosomes 21, 14 and 1 carry APP, PSEN1 and PSEN2.'],
[1,'On which cell type is TREM2, the receptor whose R47H variant raises Alzheimer\'s risk, found?',['Cholinergic neurons','Microglia','Oligodendrocytes','Red blood cells'],1,'TREM2 is a microglial receptor. Without it, microglia fail to wrap around plaques, which then have more neuritic damage around them.'],
[1,'Which pairing of gene and chromosome is correct?',['APP on chromosome 1','PSEN2 on chromosome 21','PSEN1 on chromosome 21','PSEN1 on chromosome 14'],3,'APP is on chromosome 21, PSEN1 on chromosome 14 and PSEN2 on chromosome 1.'],
[1,'In the 1997 meta-analysis of white people from clinic and autopsy series, what was the odds ratio for Alzheimer\'s with APOE ε4/ε4 compared with ε3/ε3?',['0.6','3.2','2.6','14.9'],3,'The odds ratios were 3.2 for ε3/ε4 and 14.9 for ε4/ε4, while ε2/ε3 was protective at 0.6.'],
[1,'Why do virtually all adults with Down syndrome show Alzheimer\'s pathology by age 40?',['They carry an extra copy of APP on chromosome 21','They usually carry a PSEN1 mutation','They are usually APOE ε4 homozygotes','They lack the enzyme BACE1'],0,'APP lies on chromosome 21 and is overexpressed. A woman with partial trisomy 21 and only two copies of APP showed no Alzheimer\'s disease at 78.'],
[1,'In the Rush Memory and Aging Project autopsies, what share of people with dementia had pure Alzheimer\'s pathology?',['80%','5%','30%','60%'],2,'Only 30% had pure Alzheimer\'s. More than half had several pathologies, most often Alzheimer\'s plus infarcts (38%).'],
[1,'What did Nature retract in 2024?',['The 1992 amyloid cascade hypothesis paper','The CLARITY AD trial of lecanemab','A 2006 paper reporting the Aβ*56 assembly in mice','Braak and Braak\'s 1991 staging paper'],2,'Nature retracted Lesne and colleagues\' 2006 Aβ*56 paper because the data could not be verified from the records. It concerned one oligomer species in one mouse model.'],
[1,'The Icelandic APP A673T variant sits next to the beta-secretase cut site. What does it do?',['Protects against Alzheimer\'s disease','Causes early-onset familial Alzheimer\'s','Raises ARIA risk on antibodies','Blocks tau phosphorylation'],0,'A673T reduced amyloidogenic peptide formation by about 40% in vitro and protects against Alzheimer\'s disease and age-related cognitive decline.'],
[2,'In the DIAN study of dominantly inherited Alzheimer\'s disease, which change was detected earliest?',['Episodic memory impairment','Cerebral hypometabolism','A fall in CSF Aβ42','Global cognitive impairment on the MMSE'],2,'CSF Aβ42 began to fall about 25 years before expected onset, amyloid PET about 15, hypometabolism and memory about 10, and global cognition about 5.'],
[2,'In the 2018 ATN research framework, why is the N placed in parentheses?',['N can only be measured at autopsy','N is measured only in blood','Neurodegeneration markers are not specific to Alzheimer\'s disease','N is optional when tau is normal'],2,'N (MRI, FDG-PET or CSF total tau) reflects neurodegeneration from any cause, so it is not specific to Alzheimer\'s.'],
[2,'According to the Alzheimer\'s Association 2024 criteria, what is enough to establish a diagnosis of Alzheimer\'s disease?',['Dementia plus a positive tau PET','One abnormal Core 1 biomarker','Two abnormal MMSE scores a year apart','An APOE ε4/ε4 genotype'],1,'The 2024 criteria define the disease biologically: a single abnormal Core 1 result, such as amyloid PET or an accurate plasma p-tau217 assay, is sufficient. The IWG disagrees with applying this clinically.'],
[2,'A 70-year-old with normal cognition has a positive amyloid PET and no other abnormal biomarker. Which label does the IWG 2024 recommend?',['At risk for Alzheimer\'s disease','Alzheimer\'s disease, clinical stage 4','Probable Alzheimer\'s dementia','Non-Alzheimer\'s pathologic change'],0,'The IWG recommends that amyloid-positive-only, cognitively normal people be called at risk, not described as having the disease.'],
[2,'What defines biological stage B in the Alzheimer\'s Association 2024 criteria?',['Amyloid positive with no tau PET uptake','High neocortical tau','Moderate neocortical tau','Tau PET uptake in the medial temporal lobe'],3,'Stage A is amyloid without tau PET uptake, B medial temporal tau, C moderate neocortical tau and D high neocortical tau.'],
[2,'A person\'s CDR Sum of Boxes is 6.0. Which global stage does this usually match?',['Questionable or very mild (CDR 0.5)','Moderate (CDR 2)','Mild (CDR 1)','Severe (CDR 3)'],2,'CDR-SB 4.5-9.0 corresponds to CDR 1; 0.5-4.0 to CDR 0.5, 9.5-15.5 to CDR 2 and 16.0-18.0 to CDR 3.'],
[2,'On FAST, what does substage 7c mean?',['Loss of the ability to smile','Urinary incontinence','Loss of the ability to walk','Needing help with bathing'],2,'7c is loss of walking. Loss of smiling is 7e, urinary incontinence 6d and help with bathing 6b.'],
[2,'A man can still dress himself once clothes are laid out, but needs help choosing what is appropriate to wear. Which FAST stage is most consistent?',['Stage 3','Stage 5','Stage 4','Stage 6a'],1,'Needing help choosing appropriate clothing is FAST stage 5. Stage 6a is needing help with dressing itself.'],
[2,'An MMSE score of 18 falls in which conventional severity range?',['Questionable','Mild','Severe','Moderate'],3,'Conventional ranges are 26-29 questionable, 21-25 mild, 11-20 moderate and 0-10 severe.'],
[2,'In the original MoCA validation, what share of people with MCI did a cut-off of 26 detect?',['90%','18%','50%','100%'],0,'The MoCA detected 90% of MCI and the MMSE 18%. Both detected most mild Alzheimer\'s (100% and 78%).'],
[2,'For whom did the FDA clear the Lumipulse plasma p-tau217/Aβ42 test on 16 May 2025?',['Healthy adults aged 50 and older, as a screening test','Any adult in primary care, as a stand-alone diagnosis','People with Down syndrome from age 25','Adults 55 and older with signs of cognitive decline in specialised care'],3,'The FDA states the test is not intended as a screening or stand-alone diagnostic test.'],
[2,'An 82-year-old woman with known Alzheimer\'s dementia becomes drowsy over two days and cannot keep her attention on a conversation. Her family says this is new. What is the most consistent explanation?',['Expected progression to FAST stage 7','A new diagnosis of LATE','Normal day-to-day variation in Alzheimer\'s','Delirium superimposed on dementia'],3,'Acute onset, inattention and altered consciousness are CAM features of delirium. Delirium on top of dementia is common and linked to faster decline.'],
[2,'A 74-year-old has fluctuating attention, well-formed visual hallucinations, acts out his dreams and has mild parkinsonism. Which diagnosis do these features point to?',['Typical amnestic Alzheimer\'s disease','Behavioural-variant frontotemporal dementia','Vascular dementia','Dementia with Lewy bodies'],3,'These are the four core clinical features of DLB in the 2017 consensus.'],
[2,'Gait problems came first, followed by slowed thinking and urinary urgency. MRI shows enlarged ventricles (Evans index 0.3) with tight high-convexity sulci. Which condition fits best?',['Idiopathic normal pressure hydrocephalus','Posterior cortical atrophy','Sporadic Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease','Logopenic variant PPA'],0,'In iNPH gait disturbance is the most common symptom, and imaging shows ventriculomegaly (Evans index 0.3 or more) with DESH.'],
[2,'Which pathology is most common in the logopenic variant of primary progressive aphasia?',['FTLD-TDP','Alzheimer\'s disease','FTLD-tau','Prion disease'],1,'Logopenic PPA is associated with left temporoparietal atrophy and most often Alzheimer\'s pathology, making it an atypical Alzheimer\'s presentation.'],
[2,'In a meta-analysis of 39 studies, potentially reversible causes were found in 9% of people with dementia. How many actually reversed?',['9%','25%','0.6%','56%'],2,'Only 0.6% actually reversed (0.29% partially and 0.31% fully).'],
[2,'On the Centiloid scale, what does 100 represent?',['The positivity threshold for amyloid PET','The mean of typical mild-to-moderate Alzheimer\'s disease','The highest possible amyloid load','The mean of young controls'],1,'The scale runs from 0, the mean of young controls aged 45 or under, to 100, the mean of typical mild-to-moderate Alzheimer\'s.'],
[3,'Which cholinesterase inhibitor also inhibits butyrylcholinesterase?',['Donepezil','Galantamine','Rivastigmine','Memantine'],2,'Rivastigmine is a pseudo-irreversible inhibitor of both AChE and BuChE. Donepezil is selective for AChE, and memantine is not a cholinesterase inhibitor.'],
[3,'What do the rivastigmine labels say if doses have been missed for more than 3 days?',['Restart at the lowest dose and titrate again','Restart at the previous full dose','Switch to donepezil','Give a double dose the next day'],0,'Both labels say to retitrate. The patch label cites a case of severe vomiting with oesophageal rupture after oral rivastigmine was restarted at full dose.'],
[3,'How does memantine act?',['It inhibits acetylcholinesterase','It binds soluble Aβ protofibrils','It blocks muscarinic receptors','It is an uncompetitive (open-channel) NMDA receptor antagonist'],3,'Memantine is a low-to-moderate-affinity, voltage-dependent NMDA channel blocker. Its label states no evidence that it prevents or slows neurodegeneration.'],
[3,'What did the 2019 Cochrane review find for memantine in mild Alzheimer\'s disease (MMSE 20-23)?',['No benefit','A large benefit on cognition','A benefit on agitation only','A benefit only when combined with galantamine'],0,'The review found no benefit in mild disease (ADAS-Cog difference 0.21), while finding small benefits in moderate-to-severe disease.'],
[3,'Which form of Aβ does lecanemab bind with high affinity?',['Monomers','N-terminal pyroglutamate Aβ in plaques','Phosphorylated tau','Soluble protofibrils'],3,'Lecanemab was designed to bind soluble protofibrils. Donanemab binds pyroglutamate Aβ, and solanezumab targets monomers.'],
[3,'Which form of Aβ does donanemab target?',['Soluble Aβ monomers','Soluble protofibrils','N-terminal pyroglutamate Aβ found in deposited plaque','Aβ in the blood only'],2,'Donanemab targets N3pG Aβ, a modified form found only in deposited plaque.'],
[3,'In CLARITY AD, what was the difference in CDR-SB change between lecanemab and placebo at 18 months?',['0.70 points less worsening','1.75 points less worsening','0.45 points less worsening','No difference'],2,'CDR-SB worsened by 1.21 on lecanemab versus 1.66 on placebo, a difference of 0.45 (27% slowing). 0.70 was donanemab\'s combined-population result.'],
[3,'A routine MRI during antibody treatment shows new FLAIR hyperintensity from sulcal effusion. The person has no symptoms. What is this called?',['ARIA-E','ARIA-H','Pseudo-atrophy','Superficial siderosis'],0,'ARIA-E is vasogenic oedema or sulcal effusion on FLAIR. ARIA-H is microhaemorrhage or superficial siderosis.'],
[3,'According to the labels, which group has the highest risk of ARIA on lecanemab or donanemab?',['APOE ε4 homozygotes','APOE ε4 non-carriers','APOE ε2 carriers','People with low baseline amyloid'],0,'With lecanemab, ARIA-E occurred in 32.6% of homozygotes versus 5.4% of non-carriers. Both US labels carry a boxed warning for homozygotes.'],
[3,'When does the Kisunla (donanemab) label schedule MRI scans?',['At baseline and before the 2nd, 3rd, 4th and 7th infusions','Only at baseline','Every 12 months','Only if symptoms appear'],0,'The label also calls for enhanced vigilance during the first 24 weeks, when most ARIA-E occurs.'],
[3,'Why do the antibody labels ask clinicians to consider ARIA before giving a thrombolytic for suspected stroke?',['Thrombolytics inactivate the antibody','ARIA is caused by blood clots','ARIA-E can mimic stroke, and fatal bleeds have followed thrombolysis','Thrombolytics speed up amyloid removal'],2,'Fatal haemorrhages have followed thrombolysis in antibody-treated patients, and ARIA-E can look like an ischaemic stroke.'],
[3,'In TRAILBLAZER-ALZ 2, when was donanemab switched to placebo?',['When amyloid PET showed clearance (below 11 centiloids once, or 11 to below 25 twice)','After exactly 6 infusions','When CDR-SB stopped worsening','When ARIA-H appeared'],0,'The stop-on-clearance design switched people to blinded placebo once plaque was cleared; 69% were eligible by week 76.'],
[3,'What does the boxed warning on antipsychotics for older people with dementia describe?',['A 1.6- to 1.7-fold higher risk of death','A doubling of the risk of falls only','A risk of ARIA','A risk of liver failure'],0,'Pooled analyses of 17 placebo-controlled trials showed deaths of about 4.5% versus 2.6% over 10 weeks, mostly cardiovascular or infectious.'],
[3,'What does the brexpiprazole label say about how it is used for agitation associated with Alzheimer\'s dementia?',['It is for as-needed use only','It is not for as-needed (prn) use','It has no boxed warning','It is approved for dementia-related psychosis'],1,'Brexpiprazole was approved on 11 May 2023 with a scheduled titration to 2 mg, and it keeps the class mortality warning.'],
[3,'In the DICE approach to behavioural symptoms, what does the C stand for?',['Control','Calm','Create','Chart'],2,'DICE is Describe, Investigate, Create, Evaluate: a plan is created after causes have been investigated.'],
[3,'In the CitAD trial, citalopram 30 mg a day reduced agitation. Which label point does this dose run into?',['A boxed warning for ARIA','A ban on use with donepezil','The 20 mg a day maximum for people over 60, because of QT prolongation','A minimum age of 75'],2,'QTc lengthened by 18.1 ms in CitAD, and the current label caps the dose at 20 mg a day over age 60.'],
[3,'What do the 2023 Beers Criteria say about cholinesterase inhibitors?',['Avoid them in older adults whose syncope may be due to bradycardia','Avoid them in everyone over 80','Always combine them with an anticholinergic','Use them first-line for agitation'],0,'The drugs have vagotonic effects, and a cohort study linked them to more syncope, bradycardia and pacemaker insertion.'],
[3,'In the EU, lecanemab was authorised for which group?',['APOE ε4 homozygotes only','Everyone with early Alzheimer\'s','APOE ε4 non-carriers and heterozygotes','People on anticoagulants'],2,'The EMA restricted the licence to people with one or no copy of ε4, with anticoagulation as a contraindication.'],
[4,'According to the 2024 Lancet Commission, about what share of dementia worldwide is linked to 14 modifiable risk factors?',['14%','90%','25%','45%'],3,'The weighted population attributable fraction was 45.3%, a modelled upper bound that assumes the factors are causal.'],
[4,'Which two risk factors were added in the 2024 Lancet Commission report?',['Air pollution and social isolation','Untreated vision loss and high LDL cholesterol','Hearing loss and depression','Diabetes and smoking'],1,'The other 12, including air pollution, social isolation, hearing loss and depression, were carried over from 2020.'],
[4,'In the 2024 Lancet model, which factors are the largest single contributors?',['Hearing impairment and high LDL cholesterol','Smoking and hypertension','Obesity and excessive alcohol','Diabetes and air pollution'],0,'Each accounts for about 7%. Smoking, hypertension and diabetes are about 2% each.'],
[4,'What did SPRINT MIND find for intensive blood pressure control?',['A 50% reduction in probable dementia','No significant reduction in probable dementia, but less mild cognitive impairment','More dementia in the intensive group','No effect on any cognitive outcome'],1,'Probable dementia: HR 0.83 (95% CI 0.67-1.04). MCI: HR 0.81 (0.69-0.95). The trial stopped early and may have been underpowered.'],
[4,'What was the primary result of the ACHIEVE hearing trial?',['A large benefit in everyone','No difference in 3-year cognitive change overall','Faster decline with hearing aids','Benefit only in the community volunteers'],1,'The overall result was null. A slowing of decline was seen only in the higher-risk ARIC subgroup, which supports but does not prove a benefit.'],
[4,'The Welsh shingles vaccine study used eligibility by exact date of birth. What kind of design is this?',['A double-blind randomised trial','A case-control study','A Mendelian randomisation study','A natural experiment using regression discontinuity'],3,'Uptake jumped across the birth-date cutoff, allowing a quasi-experimental comparison. No randomised trial has yet tested the vaccine for dementia.'],
[4,'On the 4AT, what does a total score of 4 or more suggest?',['Normal cognition','Possible delirium','Cognitive impairment without delirium','Severe pain'],1,'4 or more suggests delirium, 1-3 suggests cognitive impairment, and 0 does not definitively exclude either.'],
[4,'Why does the 4AT user guide warn about people with severe dementia?',['The test cannot be scored at all','They always score 0','The test takes over an hour','They may score 4 without delirium, which lowers specificity'],3,'People with severe dementia may score 2 on both AMT4 and attention. Telling delirium apart then rests on informant history and observation over time.'],
[4,'Which of these is NOT one of the five PAINAD items?',['Heart rate','Consolability','Breathing independent of vocalisation','Facial expression'],0,'PAINAD scores breathing, negative vocalisation, facial expression, body language and consolability, each 0-2.'],
[4,'According to the Exelon patch label, what is the preferred application site?',['Abdomen','Upper or lower back','Thigh','The same spot each day'],1,'The back is preferred because the person is less likely to remove it there. The abdomen and thigh give 20-30% lower exposure, and a site should not be reused within 14 days.'],
[4,'What caused most of the serious rivastigmine patch medication errors described in the label?',['The old patch was not removed, or several patches were worn at once','The patch was applied to the back','The patch was changed every 24 hours','The patch was stored in the fridge'],0,'Errors led to hospitalisation and, rarely, death. In one published case, staff applied six 9.5 mg patches on two consecutive days.'],
[4,'Under NICE SC1, when should medicines not be given covertly in a care home?',['When a family member is visiting','When the medicine is a liquid','When the resident is over 85','When the resident has capacity to decide'],3,'SC1 says covert medicines should not be given to a resident with capacity. Otherwise it calls for a capacity assessment, a best-interests meeting, documentation and review.'],
[4,'What do the letters in an ABC chart stand for?',['Airway, Breathing, Circulation','Agitation, Balance, Cognition','Antecedent, Behaviour, Consequence','Assessment, Benefit, Care plan'],2,'ABC recording captures what came before, the behaviour itself and what followed. It is the basis of functional analysis.'],
[4,'Which caregiver programme reduced the rate of nursing-home placement by 28.3%?',['Respite care in the Cochrane review','The ATTILA telecare package','The MIND diet trial','The NYU Caregiver Intervention'],3,'Counselling, support groups and on-call help for spouse caregivers delayed placement; the respite review and ATTILA showed no clear benefit.'],
[4,'What does the American Geriatrics Society (2014) say about feeding tubes in advanced dementia?',['They are recommended once eating problems start','They prevent aspiration pneumonia','They extend survival by about two years','They are not recommended; careful hand feeding is considered as good'],3,'The AGS also links tube feeding to agitation, restraint use and new pressure ulcers.'],
[4,'In the CASCADE study of nursing-home residents with advanced dementia, which complication was most common over 18 months?',['Pneumonia','Febrile episodes','Eating problems','Falls with fracture'],2,'Eating problems affected 85.8%, febrile episodes 52.6% and pneumonia 41.1%.'],
[4,'What did the A4 trial of solanezumab in cognitively unimpaired, amyloid-positive adults show?',['No slowing of cognitive decline','A 27% slowing of decline','Complete amyloid removal','High rates of ARIA-E'],0,'PACC change did not differ (P=0.26), amyloid rose in both groups, and ARIA-E was under 1%.'],
[4,'Under the Mental Capacity Act 2005, what does an unwise decision show?',['That the person lacks capacity','On its own, nothing: it does not show a lack of capacity','That a best-interests meeting is needed','That the decision must be overturned'],1,'Section 1 sets five principles, including the presumption of capacity and that an unwise decision does not show incapacity.'],
[4,'On 1 January 2025, how many drugs were in the Alzheimer\'s drug development pipeline?',['182','31','138','75'],2,'There were 138 drugs in 182 trials, and 74% were disease-targeted therapies.'],
[5,'In which year did Alois Alzheimer present the case of Auguste D. in Tübingen?',['1901','1910','1906','1968'],2,'He spoke on 3 November 1906, after Auguste D. died on 8 April 1906. She had been admitted in November 1901.'],
[5,'Who gave the disease its name, and where?',['Alois Alzheimer, in his 1907 paper','Emil Kraepelin, in his textbook Psychiatrie (1910)','Robert Katzman, in a 1976 editorial','Konrad Maurer, in a 1997 paper'],1,'Kraepelin named Alzheimer\'s disease in the 8th edition of Psychiatrie. Historians debate why he created a separate disease from so few cases.'],
[5,'What did Blessed, Tomlinson and Roth show in 1968?',['Tau is abnormally phosphorylated','APOE ε4 raises risk','Plaque counts correlated with dementia severity','Amyloid can be imaged with PET'],2,'They also found plaques and tangles in some older people without dementia.'],
[5,'What did Katzman\'s 1976 editorial argue?',['That Alzheimer\'s disease is a rare presenile illness','That Alzheimer\'s disease is common and a major killer','That plaques are harmless','That tau causes the disease'],1,'It helped reframe Alzheimer\'s as a major cause of death; US NIA research funding rose from $19.3 million in 1976 to $222.6 million in 1989.'],
[5,'Davies and Maloney (1976) reported the selective loss of which neurons?',['Dopaminergic neurons','Central cholinergic neurons','Cerebellar Purkinje cells','Spinal motor neurons'],1,'This began the cholinergic hypothesis, later set out by Bartus and colleagues in 1982.'],
[5,'What did Glenner and Wong report in 1984?',['The cloning of the APOE gene','The first tau PET tracer','The first familial APP mutation','The purification and sequence of a new cerebrovascular amyloid protein'],3,'The protein was later called amyloid-beta and had no homology to any protein known at the time.'],
[5,'What did Grundke-Iqbal and colleagues show in 1986?',['Tau in paired helical filaments is abnormally phosphorylated','Aβ42 is the main plaque component','Microglia carry TREM2','PSEN1 is on chromosome 14'],0,'Antibody labelling of tangles increased sharply after phosphatase treatment, showing the tau was abnormally phosphorylated.'],
[5,'In 1987, where did Kang and colleagues place the amyloid precursor gene?',['Chromosome 14','Chromosome 21','Chromosome 1','Chromosome 17'],1,'They cloned the 695-residue precursor, which resembles a cell-surface receptor. Goate and colleagues found the first APP mutation in 1991.'],
[5,'Who proposed the amyloid cascade hypothesis in Science in 1992?',['Braak and Braak','Hardy and Higgins','Glenner and Wong','Folstein and McHugh'],1,'It became the dominant model of Alzheimer\'s pathogenesis and remains debated.'],
[5,'What did Corder and colleagues show about APOE ε4 in 1993?',['ε4 protects against Alzheimer\'s','A gene-dose effect: more ε4 copies meant higher risk and earlier onset','ε4 causes Down syndrome','ε4 has no effect after age 60'],1,'Risk rose from 20% to 90% and mean onset fell from 84 to 68 as ε4 copies increased from 0 to 2.'],
[5,'Which drug was the first FDA-approved treatment for Alzheimer\'s disease, on 9 September 1993?',['Donepezil (Aricept)','Tacrine (Cognex)','Memantine (Namenda)','Galantamine (Razadyne)'],1,'Donepezil followed on 25 November 1996, rivastigmine in 2000, galantamine in 2001 and memantine on 16 October 2003.'],
[5,'In what year was Pittsburgh compound B first used to image amyloid in living people?',['1984','1996','2015','2004'],3,'The first human study included 16 people with mild Alzheimer\'s and 9 controls; retention was highest in frontal cortex.'],
[5,'On what basis did aducanumab receive FDA accelerated approval on 7 June 2021?',['Two positive phase 3 trials on CDR-SB','Amyloid reduction as a surrogate endpoint','A unanimous advisory committee vote in favour','Reduced mortality'],1,'The advisory committee had voted in November 2020 that the clinical evidence was insufficient; only one of the two phase 3 trials met its endpoint.'],
[5,'Which antibody was the first amyloid-directed antibody converted from accelerated to traditional FDA approval, on 6 July 2023?',['Lecanemab','Aducanumab','Donanemab','Solanezumab'],0,'The conversion followed the CLARITY AD trial. Donanemab was approved on 2 July 2024.'],
[5,'At a 1978 meeting in Bethesda, what changed?',['The first cholinesterase inhibitor was approved','The split between presenile Alzheimer\'s disease and senile dementia was dropped','Braak staging was adopted','APOE testing was recommended'],1,'Kraepelin had set Alzheimer\'s disease apart from senile dementia as a separate disease; that split between presenile Alzheimer\'s and senile dementia was dropped at the 1978 Bethesda meeting.'],
[5,'When was Auguste D.\'s case file rediscovered in Frankfurt?',['November 1906','1910','June 2021','December 1995'],3,'Konrad Maurer\'s group found the file in Frankfurt; the 1997 Lancet paper reported it.']
];
inits.quiz=()=>{
  const F=[['mix','Mixed (20)'],...Object.keys(PARTN).map(k=>[k,'Part '+k])];
  $('#qFilter').innerHTML=F.map(([k,l])=>`<button class="btn" data-qf="${k}" aria-pressed="${k==='mix'}">${l}</button>`).join('');
  let list=[],i=0,score=0,missed=[],answered=false;
  function start(k){const r=rng(Date.now()%1e6);let L=QZ.filter(q=>k==='mix'||String(q[0])===k).slice();for(let a=L.length-1;a>0;a--){const j=Math.floor(r()*(a+1));[L[a],L[j]]=[L[j],L[a]]}
    list=k==='mix'?L.slice(0,20):L;i=0;score=0;missed=[];q()}
  function q(){answered=false;$('#qBar').style.width=(i/list.length*100)+'%';
    if(i>=list.length){const pct=Math.round(score/list.length*100);
      $('#qCard').innerHTML=`<div class="kicker">Finished</div><div class="q">You scored ${score} / ${list.length} (${pct}%)</div><p>${pct>=85?'Excellent work.':pct>=65?'Solid. Review the ones you missed below.':'Worth another pass through the modules, then try again.'}</p>`+
       (missed.length?`<dl class="kv">${missed.map(m=>`<div><dt>${m[1]}</dt><dd><b>${m[2][m[3]]}.</b> ${m[4]}</dd></div>`).join('')}</dl>`:'')+`<div class="ctrls"><button class="btn primary" id="qAgain">Try again</button><button class="btn" data-go="review">Go to flashcards</button></div>`;
      $('#qAgain').onclick=()=>start($('[data-qf][aria-pressed="true"]').dataset.qf);return}
    const Q=list[i];
    $('#qCard').innerHTML=`<div class="readout"><span>question <b>${i+1} / ${list.length}</b></span><span>score <b>${score}</b></span><span>${PARTN[Q[0]]}</span></div><div class="q">${Q[1]}</div><div class="opts">${Q[2].map((o,j)=>`<button class="opt" data-o="${j}"><span class="l">${'ABCD'[j]}</span><span>${o}</span></button>`).join('')}</div><div id="qFb"></div>`;
  }
  $('#qCard').addEventListener('click',e=>{const b=e.target.closest('.opt');if(b&&!answered){answered=true;const Q=list[i],j=+b.dataset.o,ok=j===Q[3];if(ok)score++;else missed.push(Q);
      $$('.opt',$('#qCard')).forEach((o,k)=>{o.disabled=true;if(k===Q[3])o.classList.add('right');else if(k===j)o.classList.add('wrong')});
      $('#qFb').innerHTML=`<div class="expl"><b style="color:var(${ok?'--good':'--bad'})">${ok?'Correct.':'Not quite.'}</b> ${Q[4]}</div><div class="ctrls" style="margin-top:12px"><button class="btn primary" id="qNext">${i+1<list.length?'Next question →':'See results'}</button></div>`;
      $('#qNext').onclick=()=>{i++;q()};$('#qNext').focus({preventScroll:true})}});
  $('#qFilter').addEventListener('click',e=>{const b=e.target.closest('[data-qf]');if(!b)return;$$('[data-qf]').forEach(x=>x.setAttribute('aria-pressed',x===b));start(b.dataset.qf)});
  start('mix');
};

/* =============== 5.4 glossary =============== */
const GL=[
['4AT','A short delirium test with four items: Alertness, AMT4 (four orientation questions), Attention (months backwards) and Acute change or fluctuating course. Scored 0-12; 4 or more suggests delirium.','nursing'],
['ABC chart','A behaviour record of the Antecedent (what came before), the Behaviour itself and the Consequence (what followed). It is the basis of functional analysis.','nursing'],
['ABC score','The 2012 NIA-AA neuropathology score that combines A (Thal amyloid phase), B (Braak tangle stage) and C (CERAD neuritic plaque density) into Not, Low, Intermediate or High Alzheimer\'s change.','brain'],
['Aβ42/40 ratio','The ratio of Aβ42 to Aβ40 in spinal fluid or blood. An expert group recommended it over Aβ42 alone for CSF classification.','dx'],
['Acetylcholine (ACh)','A neurotransmitter made by choline acetyltransferase and broken down in the synapse by acetylcholinesterase. Cholinergic input to the cortex is progressively lost in Alzheimer\'s disease.','synapse'],
['Acetylcholinesterase (AChE)','The enzyme in the synaptic cleft that quickly breaks acetylcholine into acetate and choline. Cholinesterase inhibitors block it.','synapse'],
['ADAD','Autosomal dominant Alzheimer\'s disease, caused by mutations in APP, PSEN1 or PSEN2 or by APP duplication. Each child of a carrier has a 50% chance of inheriting the mutation.','genes'],
['ADAS-Cog','Alzheimer\'s Disease Assessment Scale, cognitive subscale. A cognition measure used in drug trials; the version in the donepezil Cochrane review runs from 0 to 70.','synapse'],
['Aducanumab','An antibody against aggregated Aβ. It received FDA accelerated approval in June 2021 based on amyloid reduction, and Biogen ended its development and sales in January 2024.','antibody'],
['Advance care planning','Discussing and recording future wishes while the person can still take part, such as lasting power of attorney, advance statements, advance decisions to refuse treatment and preferred place of care and death.','living'],
['AHEAD 3-45','A prevention programme testing lecanemab for 4 years in cognitively unimpaired people aged 55-80 with raised brain amyloid. Its primary completion is estimated for December 2028.','cure'],
['AICD','APP intracellular domain: the fragment released inside the cell when gamma-secretase cuts C83 or C99.','amyloid'],
['Amyloid cascade hypothesis','The model, proposed in 1991-1992, that build-up of Aβ is the primary driver of Alzheimer\'s disease and leads to tangles, synapse loss and dementia. It is supported by genetics and debated on several points.','amyloid'],
['Amyloid-beta (Aβ)','A peptide of 38-43 amino acids cut from APP. Aβ42 is the main plaque component and Aβ40 the most common form made.','amyloid'],
['Anticholinergic burden','The combined effect of medicines that block acetylcholine, such as some antihistamines, bladder drugs and tricyclic antidepressants. High cumulative use is associated with dementia in observational studies.','bpsd'],
['Antisense oligonucleotide','A short synthetic strand of nucleic acid that lowers production of a target protein. BIIB080 (diranersen) is a tau antisense drug given into the spinal fluid.','cure'],
['APOE','Apolipoprotein E, the strongest common genetic risk factor for late-onset Alzheimer\'s disease. Its three common alleles are ε2 (protective), ε3 (reference) and ε4 (raises risk).','genes'],
['APOE3 Christchurch','A rare APOE variant (R136S). A woman with a PSEN1 mutation and two copies of it stayed free of MCI until her seventies, about three decades later than expected.','genes'],
['APP','Amyloid precursor protein, a membrane protein encoded on chromosome 21. Aβ is cut from it by beta- and gamma-secretase.','amyloid'],
['ARIA','Amyloid-related imaging abnormalities: MRI changes seen with anti-amyloid antibodies, attributed to treatment-related loss of vessel-wall integrity. Usually without symptoms, but they can be serious or fatal.','antibody'],
['ARIA-E','ARIA with oedema or effusion: vasogenic oedema or sulcal effusion seen on FLAIR MRI. It usually resolves.','antibody'],
['ARIA-H','ARIA with haemosiderin: microhaemorrhages or superficial siderosis seen on T2* or susceptibility-weighted MRI. It persists.','antibody'],
['ATN','The 2018 research framework grouping biomarkers into A (amyloid), T (tau) and (N) neurodegeneration. N is in parentheses because it is not specific to Alzheimer\'s.','dx'],
['Auguste D.','Alois Alzheimer\'s patient, admitted in Frankfurt in November 1901. He described her brain\'s plaques and tangles in Tübingen on 3 November 1906.','history'],
['BACE1','Beta-site APP-cleaving enzyme 1 (beta-secretase). It cuts APP at the start of the Aβ sequence and is thought to be the rate-limiting step in Aβ production.','amyloid'],
['Beers Criteria','The American Geriatrics Society\'s list of potentially inappropriate medicines for older adults. The 2023 version advises avoiding benzodiazepines, Z-drugs and strong anticholinergics in dementia, and avoiding antipsychotics except in narrow circumstances.','bpsd'],
['Best interests','Under the Mental Capacity Act 2005, any act or decision for a person who lacks capacity must be in that person\'s best interests, and the less restrictive option must be considered.','nursing'],
['Biomarker cascade','The sequence in which Alzheimer\'s biomarkers turn abnormal: amyloid first, then tau and neurodegeneration markers, then symptoms.','cascade'],
['Boxed warning','The most prominent safety warning on a US drug label. Antipsychotics carry one for increased death in older people with dementia, and lecanemab and donanemab carry one for ARIA.','bpsd'],
['BPSD','Behavioural and psychological symptoms of dementia, also called neuropsychiatric symptoms: for example apathy, depression, agitation, anxiety, sleep problems and psychosis.','bpsd'],
['Braak stages','Six stages of tangle spread: I-II transentorhinal, III-IV limbic, V-VI isocortical.','brain'],
['Brexpiprazole','An antipsychotic approved by the FDA on 11 May 2023 for agitation associated with dementia due to Alzheimer\'s disease. It keeps the class mortality warning.','bpsd'],
['Butyrylcholinesterase (BuChE)','A second enzyme that breaks down acetylcholine. Its activity rises as dementia progresses. Rivastigmine inhibits it as well as AChE.','synapse'],
['C1q','A complement protein. In mouse models it builds up at synapses before plaques and tags them for removal by microglia.','glia'],
['CAA','Cerebral amyloid angiopathy: Aβ, rich in Aβ40, deposited in the walls of brain blood vessels. About 85-95% of people with Alzheimer\'s have some.','amyloid'],
['CAM','Confusion Assessment Method: delirium requires acute onset with fluctuating course and inattention, plus either disorganised thinking or an altered level of consciousness.','ddx'],
['CDR','Clinical Dementia Rating: rates six domains from an interview with the person and an informant. Global scores are 0, 0.5, 1, 2 and 3.','stages'],
['CDR-SB','CDR Sum of Boxes: the six CDR domain scores added up, from 0 to 18. It was the primary endpoint of CLARITY AD.','stages'],
['Centiloid','A standard scale for amyloid PET across tracers, from 0 (mean of young controls) to 100 (mean of typical mild-to-moderate Alzheimer\'s disease).','dx'],
['Cholinergic hypothesis','The idea, set out in 1982, that loss of cholinergic input from the basal forebrain to the cortex contributes to memory loss.','synapse'],
['Cholinesterase inhibitor','A drug that blocks the breakdown of acetylcholine: donepezil, rivastigmine and galantamine. The labels state no evidence that they change the course of the disease.','synapse'],
['CJD','Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease, a prion disease. The sporadic form causes rapidly progressive dementia, with a median of 4-5 months from onset to death.','ddx'],
['CMAI','Cohen-Mansfield Agitation Inventory: 29 agitated behaviours rated on a 7-point frequency scale. Used as an outcome in agitation trials.','bpsd'],
['Cognitive stimulation therapy (CST)','Group sessions of themed activities for people with mild to moderate dementia. NICE NG97 recommends offering it.','living'],
['Core 1 biomarker','In the Alzheimer\'s Association 2024 criteria, a marker that turns abnormal around the same time as amyloid PET: amyloid PET, approved CSF assays or accurate plasma assays such as p-tau217.','dx'],
['Covert administration','Giving medicine hidden in food or drink without the person knowing. NICE SC1 says it should not be done to a resident who has capacity.','nursing'],
['CSF','Cerebrospinal fluid, sampled by lumbar puncture. CSF Aβ42 (or the Aβ42/40 ratio) falls and phosphorylated tau rises in Alzheimer\'s disease.','dx'],
['Delirium superimposed on dementia (DSD)','Delirium occurring in a person who already has dementia. It is common, often missed, and linked to faster decline.','nursing'],
['DIAN','Dominantly Inherited Alzheimer Network: a study of families with APP, PSEN1 or PSEN2 mutations that timed biomarker changes against expected age of onset.','cascade'],
['DICE','Describe, Investigate, Create, Evaluate: a caregiver-centred approach to behavioural symptoms that looks for causes before drugs.','bpsd'],
['Disease-associated microglia (DAM)','A microglial state containing Aβ, identified by single-cell mapping. It switches on in two steps, the second dependent on TREM2.','glia'],
['Disease-targeted therapy (DTT)','A drug aimed at the underlying disease process rather than symptoms. The pipeline authors prefer this term to disease-modifying, which they call aspirational.','cure'],
['DLB','Dementia with Lewy bodies: core features are fluctuating cognition, recurrent visual hallucinations, REM sleep behaviour disorder and parkinsonism.','ddx'],
['Donanemab','An antibody against N-terminal pyroglutamate Aβ in plaques (brand name Kisunla), approved by the FDA on 2 July 2024. Dosing can stop once amyloid is cleared.','antibody'],
['Donepezil','A selective acetylcholinesterase inhibitor taken once daily, with a half-life of about 70 hours. It is labelled for mild, moderate and severe Alzheimer\'s dementia.','synapse'],
['Down syndrome','Trisomy 21. Because APP is on chromosome 21, virtually all adults with Down syndrome show Alzheimer\'s pathology by age 40; this is now described as a genetic form of Alzheimer\'s.','genes'],
['Elderspeak','Infantilising talk to older people. In videotaped nursing-home care it was followed by resistance to care more often than normal talk.','nursing'],
['Entorhinal cortex','The cortex that relays information into and out of the hippocampus. Its layer II neurons are lost early in Alzheimer\'s disease.','brain'],
['Episodic memory','Memory for personal events and recent conversations. It is usually the first ability affected in typical Alzheimer\'s disease.','brain'],
['FAST','Functional Assessment Staging: rates functional decline over the whole course of Alzheimer\'s, from stage 1 to 7, with substages 6a-6e and 7a-7f.','stages'],
['FDG-PET','PET imaging of glucose use in the brain. Reduced posterior cingulate uptake with relatively preserved occipital uptake is typical of Alzheimer\'s.','dx'],
['FINGER','A Finnish trial of a 2-year multidomain lifestyle programme (diet, exercise, cognitive training, vascular monitoring) in at-risk older adults. It found a small cognitive benefit.','prevent'],
['Galantamine','A competitive, reversible acetylcholinesterase inhibitor with a half-life of about 7 hours, labelled for mild-to-moderate Alzheimer\'s dementia.','synapse'],
['Gamma-secretase','A four-part enzyme complex with presenilin at its core. It cuts APP fragments inside the membrane to release Aβ.','amyloid'],
['GFAP','Glial fibrillary acidic protein, a blood marker of astrocyte activation. It is raised in amyloid-positive people but is not used for diagnosis.','dx'],
['Glymphatic system','A proposed route by which spinal fluid flows through the brain along blood vessels and clears waste such as Aβ, especially during sleep. The evidence is mainly from rodents and is mixed.','glia'],
['GUIDE model','Guiding an Improved Dementia Experience: an 8-year US Medicare model launched on 1 July 2024 offering care navigation, caregiver training, a 24/7 support line and respite.','living'],
['Hippocampus','A medial temporal lobe structure needed to form new episodic memories. Its atrophy on MRI is a marker of Alzheimer\'s disease.','brain'],
['Hyperphosphorylation','Excess phosphate groups added to tau. Hyperphosphorylated tau detaches from microtubules and assembles into tangles.','tau'],
['iADRS','Integrated Alzheimer\'s Disease Rating Scale (0-144, lower is worse), combining cognition and daily function. It was the primary endpoint of TRAILBLAZER-ALZ 2.','antibody'],
['IWG','International Working Group. Its 2024 recommendation treats Alzheimer\'s as a clinical-biological construct and calls most biomarker-positive people without symptoms at risk, not as having the disease.','dx'],
['LATE','Limbic-predominant age-related TDP-43 encephalopathy: a TDP-43 disease of older adults that causes an amnestic dementia resembling Alzheimer\'s.','ddx'],
['Lecanemab','An antibody that binds soluble Aβ protofibrils (brand name Leqembi). FDA traditional approval came on 6 July 2023 after the CLARITY AD trial.','antibody'],
['Logopenic variant PPA','A primary progressive aphasia with word-finding pauses and impaired sentence repetition. Its most common pathology is Alzheimer\'s disease.','ddx'],
['MAPT','The gene for tau, on chromosome 17.','tau'],
['MCI','Mild cognitive impairment: measurable cognitive decline with daily activities largely preserved. Many people with MCI do not progress to dementia.','stages'],
['Memantine','An uncompetitive NMDA receptor antagonist labelled for moderate-to-severe Alzheimer\'s dementia.','synapse'],
['Mental Capacity Act 2005','The law in England and Wales on decisions for people who may lack capacity. It starts from a presumption of capacity and sets five principles.','nursing'],
['Microglia','The brain\'s resident immune cells. They cluster around plaques and can prune synapses.','glia'],
['Mini-Cog','A brief screen combining three-word recall with a clock drawing, scored 0-5. It takes about 3 minutes.','dx'],
['Mixed pathology','More than one cause of dementia in the same brain, such as Alzheimer\'s plus infarcts or Lewy bodies. A 2024 history review calls it the most frequent cause of dementia in the oldest old.','what'],
['MMSE','Mini-Mental State Examination: an 11-item, 30-point cognitive screen. It is affected by age and education and should not be used alone to diagnose.','dx'],
['MoCA','Montreal Cognitive Assessment: a 10-minute, 30-point screen designed for MCI. The cut-off is disputed: 26 in the original validation, 23 in a 2018 meta-analysis.','dx'],
['Neuritic plaque','A dense-core amyloid plaque surrounded by swollen, tau-positive neurites, activated microglia and reactive astrocytes.','amyloid'],
['Neurofibrillary tangle (NFT)','An aggregate of hyperphosphorylated tau filaments inside a neuron, flame-shaped in pyramidal cells.','tau'],
['NfL','Neurofilament light, a blood and spinal fluid marker of nerve damage from any cause. It is not specific to Alzheimer\'s.','dx'],
['NMDA receptor','A glutamate receptor channel. Memantine blocks it when it is excessively open.','synapse'],
['NPI','Neuropsychiatric Inventory: a caregiver interview scoring the frequency and severity of behavioural and psychological symptoms.','bpsd'],
['Nucleus basalis of Meynert','A basal forebrain nucleus that is the main source of acetylcholine for the cortex. More than 75% of its neurons are lost in Alzheimer\'s.','brain'],
['Oligomer','A small soluble cluster of Aβ molecules. Reviews propose that oligomers, more than plaques, drive synapse damage.','amyloid'],
['p-tau217','Tau phosphorylated at position 217. In plasma it is one of the most accurate blood markers of Alzheimer\'s pathology.','dx'],
['PAINAD','Pain Assessment in Advanced Dementia: an observation scale of breathing, negative vocalisation, facial expression, body language and consolability, scored 0-10.','nursing'],
['Paired helical filament (PHF)','The main tau filament in tangles: two filaments twisted around each other.','tau'],
['Person-centred care','Care organised around the person\'s identity, history and preferences rather than tasks. Associated with Kitwood.','nursing'],
['PET','Positron emission tomography: imaging with radioactive tracers that bind amyloid or tau or show glucose use.','dx'],
['Pittsburgh compound B (PiB)','The first amyloid PET tracer studied in people, reported in 2004 in 16 people with mild Alzheimer\'s disease and 9 controls.','history'],
['Population attributable fraction (PAF)','The share of cases that would theoretically be avoided if a risk factor were removed, assuming it is causal.','prevent'],
['Posterior cortical atrophy','An atypical presentation, usually caused by Alzheimer\'s pathology, with problems in visual and spatial perception rather than memory.','stages'],
['Prion-like spread','The idea that misfolded tau seeds pass from neuron to connected neuron and template misfolding there.','tau'],
['Protofibril','A curved, soluble intermediate between Aβ oligomers and mature fibrils. Lecanemab binds it with high affinity.','amyloid'],
['PSEN1','Presenilin 1, on chromosome 14, the catalytic part of gamma-secretase. It has the most known pathogenic variants, and its mutations give the earliest onsets.','genes'],
['PSEN2','Presenilin 2, on chromosome 1. Its mutations cause autosomal dominant Alzheimer\'s with a wide range of onset ages.','genes'],
['Pseudo-atrophy','Extra brain volume loss on MRI after amyloid-lowering antibodies. Some authors attribute it to plaque removal; others read it as possible harm.','antibody'],
['Regression discontinuity','A natural-experiment design that compares people just either side of an eligibility cutoff, such as a birth date for shingles vaccination.','prevent'],
['Respite care','Short breaks for family caregivers. A Cochrane review found no clear benefit or harm, mainly because of a lack of good studies.','living'],
['Rivastigmine','A pseudo-irreversible inhibitor of both AChE and BuChE, given as capsules or a daily skin patch.','synapse'],
['Superficial siderosis','Blood-derived iron (haemosiderin) deposited on the surface of the brain, seen on T2* or susceptibility-weighted MRI. With antibodies it counts as ARIA-H.','antibody'],
['Synapse loss','Loss of connections between neurons. Neocortical synapse density correlated most strongly with cognition in a classic 1991 study.','glia'],
['Tau','A neuronal protein that stabilises microtubules. In Alzheimer\'s it becomes hyperphosphorylated and forms tangles.','tau'],
['Tau PET','PET imaging of tau tangles, used for biological staging in the 2024 criteria.','dx'],
['TDP-43','A protein that forms inclusions in LATE and in some frontotemporal dementias.','ddx'],
['Thal phases','Five phases of amyloid spread: neocortex, allocortex, then diencephalon, striatum and basal forebrain, then brainstem, then cerebellum.','brain'],
['TREM2','A receptor on microglia. Rare variants such as R47H raise Alzheimer\'s risk.','glia'],
['Trontinemab','An anti-amyloid antibody fused to a transferrin-receptor brain shuttle that carries it across the blood-brain barrier. Phase 3 trials started in late 2025.','cure'],
['Vascular dementia','Dementia caused by cerebrovascular disease, about 15% of cases. Its course can be stepwise or variable, and diagnosis needs imaging evidence of cerebrovascular disease.','ddx'],
['Young-onset dementia','Dementia beginning before age 65. Early-onset Alzheimer\'s is about 5% of all Alzheimer\'s disease, and most cases are not familial.','what']
];
inits.glossary=()=>{
  const inp=$('#glSearch');const tname=id=>{const m=MODS.find(x=>x.id===id);return m?m.t:''};
  function render(){const q=inp.value.trim().toLowerCase();const L=GL.filter(g=>!q||g[0].toLowerCase().includes(q)||g[1].toLowerCase().includes(q));
    $('#glList').innerHTML=L.map(g=>`<div><b>${g[0]}</b>${g[1]} <button class="btn ghost" style="padding:3px 9px;font-size:12px;margin-top:6px" data-go="${g[2]}">→ ${tname(g[2])}</button></div>`).join('')||'<p>No matching terms.</p>';
    $('#glCount').textContent=`${L.length} of ${GL.length} terms`}
  inp.addEventListener('input',render);render();
};

/* =============== 5.5 sources =============== */
const AREAN={A:'Epidemiology, stages & history',B:'Pathology',C:'Genetics',D:'Diagnosis & biomarkers',E:'Symptomatic drugs & BPSD',F:'Anti-amyloid antibodies',G:'Prevention, living & caregiving',H:'Nursing care',I:'Research & cure'};
inits.sources=()=>{
  let area='all';const inp=$('#srcSearch');
  $('#srcFilter').innerHTML=[['all','All']].concat(Object.entries(AREAN)).map(([k,l])=>`<button class="btn" data-sa="${k}" aria-pressed="${k==='all'}">${l}</button>`).join('');
  const esc=s=>s.replace(/&/g,'&amp;').replace(/</g,'&lt;').replace(/>/g,'&gt;');
  function render(){const q=inp.value.trim().toLowerCase();let n=0;
    $('#srcList').innerHTML=SOURCES.map((s,i)=>{const show=(area==='all'||s.areas.includes(area))&&(!q||s.text.toLowerCase().includes(q)||s.key.toLowerCase().includes(q));if(!show)return'';n++;
      return `<li id="src-${s.key}" value="${i+1}"><span class="key">[${i+1}] ${esc(s.key)}${s.aliases?' · also '+s.aliases.map(esc).join(', '):''}</span><span>${esc(s.text)}</span>${s.link?`<a href="${esc(s.link)}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">${esc(s.link)}</a>`:''}${s.note?`<span class="srcnote">${esc(s.note)}</span>`:''}<span class="mods">${s.areas.map(a=>`<span class="tag sea">${AREAN[a]}</span>`).join('')}${s.unverified?'<span class="tag warn">check</span>':''}</span></li>`}).join('');
    $('#srcCount').textContent=`${n} of ${SOURCES.length} sources`}
  inp.addEventListener('input',render);
  $('#srcFilter').addEventListener('click',e=>{const b=e.target.closest('[data-sa]');if(!b)return;area=b.dataset.sa;$$('[data-sa]').forEach(x=>x.setAttribute('aria-pressed',x===b));render()});
  render();
};
onShow.sources=()=>{const inp=$('#srcSearch');if(inp&&inp.value){inp.value='';inp.dispatchEvent(new Event('input'))}
  if($('[data-sa="all"]')&&$('[data-sa="all"]').getAttribute('aria-pressed')!=='true')$('[data-sa="all"]').click()};
